Sampled Evidence: Deaths and Pumps

deaths &lt;- as_tibble(Snow.deaths) |&gt; mutate(did = row_number())
pumps  &lt;- as_tibble(Snow.pumps)  |&gt; mutate(pid = row_number())

# Every death-pump distance, then each death's NEAREST pump
pairs &lt;- crossing(did = deaths$did, pid = pumps$pid) |&gt;
  left_join(deaths |&gt; select(did, dx = x, dy = y), by = "did") |&gt;
  left_join(pumps  |&gt; select(pid, px = x, py = y), by = "pid") |&gt;
  mutate(dist = sqrt((dx - px)^2 + (dy - py)^2))

nearest &lt;- pairs |&gt;
  slice_min(dist, n = 1, by = did)

by_pump &lt;- nearest |&gt;
  count(pid, name = "deaths_nearest") |&gt;
  arrange(desc(deaths_nearest))
by_pump

The Classic Picture: 100 Samples, 100 Intervals

ci_100 &lt;- map(1:100, \(i) {
  s  &lt;- crimes |&gt; slice_sample(n = 250)
  ci &lt;- prop.test(sum(s$arrest), n = 250)$conf.int
  tibble(sample = i, estimate = mean(s$arrest), lo = ci[1], hi = ci[2])
}) |&gt;
  list_rbind() |&gt;
  mutate(captured = lo &lt;= mean(crimes$arrest) & mean(crimes$arrest) &lt;= hi)

sum(ci_100$captured)   # how many of the 100 intervals caught the truth?

Conditionals Reveal What Variables Measure

crimes |&gt;
  filter(primary_type == "NARCOTICS") |&gt;
  summarize(p_arrest = mean(arrest), n = n())

# A tibble: 1 × 2
  p_arrest     n
     &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;int&gt;
1    0.971   919


P(arrest | narcotics) ≈ 0.97. Is Chicago spectacular at solving drug crimes?

No — a narcotics record usually exists because someone was arrested. Conditional probabilities describe the data-generating process, not just the world. Always ask how did this row get into the data?

